<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hubei</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hubei is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>. In antiquity it was the heartland of the State of Chu, whose capital Ying lay near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingzhou">Jingzhou</a>, and it has yielded many Chu tombs and bamboo texts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a> there in 1911 began the revolution that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a> Dam lies in its west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hubei.jpg" alt="Hubei" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fredlyfish4 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiling_Gorge_2016_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son King Wen (r. 689–677 BCE) moved the capital to Ying, usually identified with the walled site of Ji'nancheng near Jingzhou in <b>Hubei</b>, although the early Ying may have lain elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bamboo-slip documents excavated at Shuihudi in <b>Hubei</b> in 1975 and at Liye in Hunan in 2002 reveal a bureaucracy that recorded granaries, tools, labour service and lawsuits in minute detail and held officials strictly accountable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…known manuscripts, which show that the text was still fluid, come from tombs: bamboo slips from Guodian in <b>Hubei</b>, buried around 300 BCE and found in 1993, contain parts of it, and two silk manuscripts from Mawangdui in Hunan, buried in 168 BCE and found in 1973, contain…</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>First centred on Henan and Shanxi, it later spread to Shaanxi and <b>Hubei</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Zuo Zongtang founded the Fuzhou Navy Yard before leaving to fight in the north-west, where he later reconquered Xinjiang; Shen Baozhen directed the yard after him; and Zhang Zhidong built industries in <b>Hubei</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Bamboo strips acquired by Anhui University in 2015, the “Zhongni Said”, and the “Kongzi Said” strips recovered from a tomb at Wangjiazui in <b>Hubei</b> between 2019 and 2021 both date to around 300 BCE, confirming that Confucius's sayings were being preserved and organised by the mid-Warring States period.</blockquote><a href="/work/analects">Analects · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
